  "summary": "Shining the Holy Ark is Sonic! Software Planning and Camelot's role-playing adventure, first released for Saturn in Japan on 20 December 1996.",
  "body": [
    "Shining the Holy Ark is Sonic! Software Planning and Camelot's role-playing adventure, first released for Saturn in Japan on 20 December 1996. It returns the Shining series to first-person dungeon exploration and turn-based battles, following a party through a story involving the Vandals and Innovators. The player sees towns and passages from the adventurers' viewpoint, moving between fixed positions rather than freely across the floor. Polygons and sprites give the spaces depth while keeping encounters close to the screen, where approaching creatures become an immediate interruption to exploration.",
    "Up to four active characters form the party, with additional companions held in reserve and available during battle. Commands use the series' four-option icon menus, making attacks, magic and other decisions accessible through a consistent arrangement. Experience improves the characters, new levels provide spells, and better equipment changes their effectiveness. Battles retain the first-person view but show allies as they act, briefly bringing the people behind that shared viewpoint into sight. Managing the group therefore means considering both the next command and which companions should be facing the encounter.",
    "Pixies found during the journey can attack at the beginning of a battle. Different types respond to enemies arriving from different directions: a fairy can meet a threat from above, while a leprechaun answers one emerging below. The player has a short opportunity to choose the appropriate helper. Getting it right damages the enemy and increases the rewards, giving exploration a useful collection to build. Motoi Sakuraba's music accompanies these passages and encounters, while the pixie system adds a quick moment of observation before the more deliberate decisions of turn-based combat."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Looking into the dungeon",
    "A party behind one viewpoint",
    "A small opening before the battle"
  ],
